+[ 96%] Linking CXX shared library ../../lib/libvisp_rbt.so
+[ 96%] Built target visp_rbt
+[ 96%] Installing the python bindings generator and running it...
+error: externally-managed-environment
+
+× This environment is externally managed
+╰─> To install Python packages system-wide, try apt install
+    python3-xyz, where xyz is the package you are trying to
+    install.
+
+    If you wish to install a non-Debian-packaged Python package,
+    create a virtual environment using python3 -m venv path/to/venv.
+    Then use path/to/venv/bin/python and path/to/venv/bin/pip. Make
+    sure you have python3-full installed.
+
+    If you wish to install a non-Debian packaged Python application,
+    it may be easiest to use pipx install xyz, which will manage a
+    virtual environment for you. Make sure you have pipx installed.
+
+    See /usr/share/doc/python3.12/README.venv for more information.
+
+note: If you believe this is a mistake, please contact your Python installation or OS distribution provider.
+You can override this, at the risk of breaking your Python installation or OS, by passing --break-system-packages.
+hint: See PEP 668 for the detailed specification.
+make[3]: *** [modules/python/CMakeFiles/visp_python_bindings_generator_run.dir/build.make:145: modules/python/bindings/src/main.cpp] Error 1
+make[2]: *** [CMakeFiles/Makefile2:28000: modules/python/CMakeFiles/visp_python_bindings_generator_run.dir/all] Error 2
+make[1]: *** [CMakeFiles/Makefile2:28033: modules/python/CMakeFiles/visp_python_bindings.dir/rule] Error 2
+make: *** [Makefile:8356: visp_python_bindings] Error 2
+\endcode
+
+Although you are working within a Conda environment, the ViSP build script is attempting to invoke the system's pip.
+This error indicates that CMake is likely configured to use the system Python (`/usr/bin/python3`) instead of the
+interpreter from your `visp-conda-ws` environment.
+
+You can verify this by inspecting the `ViSP-third-party.txt` file, where you will likely find the following
+configuration:
+\code{.sh}(visp-conda-ws) .../visp/visp-build-bindings$ cat ViSP-third-party.txt
+
+==========================================================
+General configuration information for ViSP 3.7.1
+
+  Version control:               v3.7.0-128-g853626f3f
+
+  Platform:
+    ...
+
+  System information:
+    ...
+
+  C/C++:
+    ...
+
+  ViSP modules:
+    ...
+
+  Python 3:
+    Interpreter:                 /usr/bin/python3 (ver 3.12.3)
+
+  Java:
+    ...
+
+  Python3 bindings:              yes
+    Python3 interpreter:         /usr/bin/python3 (ver 3.12.3)               <-- Currently using system Python
+    Pybind11:                    /usr/lib/cmake/pybind11 (2.11.1)            <-- Currently using system Pybind11
+    Package version:             3.7.1
+    Wrapped modules:             core dnn_tracker gui imgproc io klt me sensor ar blob robot visual_features vs vision detection mbt rbt tt tt_mi
+    Generated input config:      /home/fspindle/soft/visp-ws/visp/visp-build-bindings/modules/python/cmake_config.json
+\endcode
+
+As shown above, both the Python3 interpreter and Pybind11 are being sourced from the system paths rather than the
+Conda environment. This confirms that CMake is not yet pointing to your virtual environment.
+
+To resolve this issue, as noted in section \ref py_bindings_build_conda, ensure that Python and its development
+tools are correctly installed within your Conda environment. After installing the required dependencies with:
+
+\code{.sh}
+(visp-conda-ws) $ conda install python pybind11
+\endcode
+
+And re-running CMake while pointing to your environment's prefix:
+
+\code{.sh}
+(visp-conda-ws) $ cmake ../visp -DCMAKE_PREFIX_PATH=$CONDA_PREFIX -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$CONDA_PREFIX
+\endcode
+
+You should see that both the `Python interpreter` and `Pybind11` are now correctly sourced from the Conda environment:
+
+\code{.sh}
+  Python3 bindings:               yes
+    Python3 interpreter:          $HOME/miniforge3/envs/visp-conda-ws/bin/python3.14 (ver 3.14.3)
+    Pybind11:                     $HOME/miniforge3/envs/visp-conda-ws/share/cmake/pybind11 (3.0.2)
+\endcode
+
